From 3269003577acaf923607e3dfa844e755faff0c38 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:52:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 137/274] DeepSeek-V4.1 Engram: read disk rows with a native
 worker; one wait per table before the gather

Disk Engram rows were read by a Python thread that waited on a CUDA event for
the row IDs, released a host gate that a side stream waited on, and a side-stream
B12X lookup set a flag the forward's graph waited on. The reader needed the GIL,
so any main-thread call that held the GIL while waiting for the device deadlocked
the step (spark-ds41f experiments/2026-10-15-engram-stall).

Per step now:
- the model's stream copies each table's row IDs to pinned host memory and writes
  the step's sequence number into the table's control block in mapped host memory;
- one native worker (engram_rows/_reader.c) serves every table in staging order,
  so the first Engram layer's rows are never held back by the second table's
  reads. It sees the IDs, reads this rank's rows from the checkpoint into mapped
  buffers and publishes the step. It never takes the GIL or calls CUDA. A failed
  read is recorded, never published, and marked in the control block;
- in the Engram layer's forward one thread waits for the table's step, bounded
  (SPARK3_ENGRAM_ROWS_TIMEOUT_MS, 10 s). A timed-out wait or a failed read records
  the step and the time waited and stops the GPU, so no kernel reads stale rows.
  Then a TileLang gather dequantizes this rank's rows as B12X's lookup did, bit for
  bit, and writes zeros for other ranks' rows without loading their slots.

One wait per table, not one per gather block: polling host memory from every
block cost about 0.5 us a block (100 ms per table of an 8K prefill chunk). Against
B12X on the same data the gather is within about 1 us at 16-96 tokens and faster
from 512 tokens, and the two tables' reads are level at decode sizes and faster at
prefill sizes (spark-ds41f experiments/2026-10-15-engram-rows-v2).

Gone: the Python reader thread and its executor, the side stream, the CUDA
events, the rows flag and host gate with their stream waits, the wait custom op,
the synchronous B12X batch path, the B12X lookup plan for disk tables, the
zero-filling of other ranks' slots, and SPARK3_ENGRAM_ASYNC. disk_resident_scales
goes with the path that supported it; EngramConfig still accepts it as False, so
deployments that pass it keep starting, and rejects True.
SPARK3_ENGRAM_READ_DELAY_MS delays every read, for tests.

Request planning and io_uring execution come from B12X's PLE reader (Apache-2.0),
sorted, deduplicated, 4 KiB-aligned O_DIRECT reads, coalesced up to 64 KiB,
scattered to lookup slots; a short read continues, and only the end of the file
fails a read. The worker spins 200 us for a step's IDs, then polls every 10 us
with a 1 ns timer slack. The extension builds at first use with the system
compiler and liburing, cached by source digest.

